{"url": "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=distinction+between+moral+agency+and+functional+accountability+in+autonomous+systems+frameworks&format=json&resultType=core&pageSize=4", "scope": "Europe PMC search metadata, abstracts and full-text links; discovery only", "excerpt": "[{"title": "Cared for But Not Cared About? AI and the Moral Practice of Nursing.", "abstract": "Artificial intelligence is increasingly deployed in healthcare not only to provide information or perform clinical tasks, but also to offer reassurance, companionship, and emotional support. This paper introduces the concept of engineered care to describe technological practices designed to generate the experiential effects of caregiving through system design rather than through the relational and moral practices that have traditionally constituted care. Its characteristic product is engineered comfort: the felt experience of reassurance, support, or being attended to that such systems can produce without assuming responsibility for the person receiving it. This comfort can be meaningful and may reduce distress, particularly where human care is scarce. The ethical challenge, therefore, is not simply that these systems fail, but that they succeed in producing experiences that resemble care. Drawing on care ethics, particularly Tronto's account of care as a moral practice, and on nursing's relational commitments, the paper argues that AI systems' growing capacity to produce comforting experiences risks redefining care as the delivery of desirable emotional outcomes. As reassurance and support become proxies for care, experiential indicators of success displace relational practices. Relational labor is increasingly delegated to technological systems, while responsibility for addressing the emotional and relational needs those systems leave unresolved continues to fall on nurses. Distinguishing engineered comfort from care enables nursing ethics to acknowledge these technologies' benefits without treating their experiential effects as equivalent to moral and relational practice.", "doi": "10.1111/nup.70135", "pmid": "42929698", "pmcid": "PMC13701077", "source": "MED", "fullTextUrlList": {"fullTextUrl": [{"availability": "Subscription required", "availabilityCode": "S", "documentStyle": "doi", "site": "DOI", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1111/nup.70135"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "html", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13701077"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "pdf", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13701077?pdf=render"}]}}, {"title": "The foundational role of autonomy for brain-computer interfaces.", "abstract": "

